-- Q1: hard / L4 — Evaluating whether internal audit work can reduce external auditor control testing
(1, 'Tests of Controls', $EXPL$Morrison & Blake, CPAs, are auditing Fairview Industries and plan to rely on controls over inventory. Fairview has a competent internal audit function that tested the same inventory controls during the year. Under AU-C 610, the external auditor is considering using the internal auditors' work to reduce the extent of direct testing. Which factor is most critical in making this determination?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Whether the internal audit function reports to the chief financial officer rather than the audit committee", "Whether the internal auditors used statistical sampling rather than non-statistical sampling", "The degree of subjectivity involved in evaluating the audit evidence and the assessed risk of material misstatement in the area", "Whether the internal auditors hold the Certified Internal Auditor designation"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): AU-C 610.A12. The external auditor may use internal audit work to reduce direct testing, but must consider the degree of subjectivity in evaluating evidence and the assessed risk level. Higher subjectivity and higher risk require more direct testing by the external auditor, limiting reliance on internal audit work. Wrong (A): While reporting structure affects objectivity assessment, it is not the most critical factor for determining the extent of reliance on specific control testing work. Wrong (B): The sampling method used is a procedural detail; the critical factor is the judgment and risk level involved. Wrong (D): Professional certifications may support competence but are not the primary determinant of how much work can be used.$EXPL$, 'hard', 4),

-- Q2: easy / L1 — Definition of upper deviation limit
(1, 'Tests of Controls', $EXPL$After completing a statistical sample for a test of controls, the auditor calculates the upper deviation limit. This metric represents:$EXPL$, $EXPL$["The minimum number of deviations the auditor expects to find in the sample", "The highest estimated deviation rate in the population at the desired confidence level", "The percentage of the population that was not tested due to time constraints", "The total number of control deviations found across all audit areas"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): AU-C 530.A20. The upper deviation limit (also called the achieved upper precision limit) is the highest estimated population deviation rate at the specified confidence level based on the sample results. If the upper deviation limit exceeds the tolerable rate, the auditor cannot rely on the control as planned. Wrong (A): The expected deviation rate is determined before sampling, not after; the upper deviation limit is a post-sampling evaluation. Wrong (C): This describes an incomplete testing limitation, not a statistical measure of deviation rates. Wrong (D): The upper deviation limit is a rate estimate for the population, not a raw count of deviations across areas.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q3: easy / L2 — Identifying a situation requiring 100% testing of control occurrences
(1, 'Tests of Controls', $EXPL$Thornton Analytics performs a quarterly reconciliation of its investment portfolio (4 times per year). The auditor plans to rely on this control. When determining the sample size for testing this control, the auditor should recognize that:$EXPL$, $EXPL$["A random sample of 2 occurrences is sufficient for quarterly controls", "The auditor must test all 4 occurrences because the population is too small for meaningful sampling", "The auditor should test only the year-end reconciliation because it is the most recent", "Quarterly controls do not need to be tested because they operate too infrequently to provide reliable evidence"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): AU-C 530.A14. When a control operates only a few times during the period (such as quarterly), the population is too small for sampling to be meaningful. The auditor should test each occurrence to obtain sufficient evidence of operating effectiveness throughout the period. Wrong (A): Testing only 2 of 4 occurrences does not provide adequate coverage for such a small population. Wrong (C): Testing only the year-end occurrence ignores three-quarters of the period and provides insufficient evidence of consistent operation. Wrong (D): Quarterly controls can provide reliable evidence when all occurrences are tested; infrequent operation does not render them unreliable.$EXPL$, 'easy', 2),

-- Q4: easy / L1 — Nature of detective controls
(1, 'Tests of Controls', $EXPL$A bank reconciliation prepared by the controller that identifies and investigates unusual items is an example of:$EXPL$, $EXPL$["A preventive control", "An IT general control", "A detective control", "A corrective control"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): AU-C 315.A81. Detective controls identify errors or irregularities after they have occurred rather than preventing them. A bank reconciliation discovers discrepancies between recorded amounts and bank records after transactions have been processed, making it a detective control. Wrong (A): Preventive controls stop errors before they occur, such as authorization requirements or segregation of duties. Wrong (B): IT general controls relate to the IT environment (access security, change management), not manual reconciliation procedures. Wrong (D): Corrective controls remedy identified errors; while the reconciliation may lead to corrections, the reconciliation itself is the detective mechanism.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q5: medium / L3 — Applying the concept of unpredictability in control testing
(1, 'Tests of Controls', $EXPL$During the audit of Redstone Electronics, the engagement partner instructs the team to incorporate an element of unpredictability into their tests of controls. Which of the following actions best achieves this objective?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Testing controls at unexpected times or extending procedures to areas not normally tested in prior years", "Informing client personnel in advance about which controls will be examined so they can prepare documentation", "Testing the same controls at the same interim date used in the prior three years for consistency", "Selecting only the largest transactions for testing because they present the highest risk"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): AU-C 240.A39. Incorporating unpredictability may include performing procedures at unexpected times, extending procedures to locations or accounts not ordinarily selected, or varying the timing and method of testing. This reduces the risk that personnel who are aware of audit routines could conceal fraud. Wrong (B): Advance notification of specific testing plans is the opposite of unpredictability and may allow personnel to prepare or conceal issues. Wrong (C): Using the same timing every year creates predictability and reduces the deterrent effect of audit procedures. Wrong (D): Selecting only the largest items is a risk-based approach, not an element of unpredictability; it follows a predictable pattern.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q6: hard / L4 — Evaluating control testing in a first-year audit
(1, 'Tests of Controls', $EXPL$Davis & Co. is performing a first-year audit of Colton Enterprises. The prior auditor tested controls over revenue recognition and found them effective. Davis plans to rely on the same controls. Since Davis has no prior experience with this client, what is the most appropriate approach to testing these controls?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Review the prior auditor's workpapers and rely on their control testing conclusions without additional testing", "Test all controls the auditor plans to rely on in the current year, regardless of the prior auditor's results", "Reduce current-year sample sizes based on the prior auditor's favorable results", "Perform inquiry of management about whether controls have changed and accept their representations"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): AU-C 330.14. In a first-year engagement, the auditor cannot rotate or reduce testing based on prior-year results from a predecessor auditor. The current auditor must independently test all controls they plan to rely on because they have no firsthand basis for assessing control continuity or the predecessor's work quality. Wrong (A): Reviewing predecessor workpapers may provide useful background but does not substitute for the current auditor's own testing of operating effectiveness. Wrong (C): Reducing sample sizes based on another auditor's work is inappropriate when the current auditor has no established basis for reliance. Wrong (D): Management inquiry alone is never sufficient to test operating effectiveness, especially in a first-year engagement where the auditor lacks historical context.$EXPL$, 'hard', 4),

-- Q7: medium / L2 — Identifying the effect of deviations on assessed risk
(1, 'Tests of Controls', $EXPL$During the audit of Hartwell Industries, the auditor tested 60 cash disbursement authorizations and found 5 deviations, yielding an 8.3% sample deviation rate. The tolerable deviation rate was set at 5%. The auditor should conclude that:$EXPL$, $EXPL$["The assessed risk of material misstatement should be reduced because 55 of 60 items were properly authorized", "The control is likely not operating effectively and the assessed risk of material misstatement for cash disbursements should be increased", "The 8.3% rate is acceptable because it falls within a reasonable margin above the tolerable rate", "The deviations should be offset against favorable results from other control tests in the disbursement cycle"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): AU-C 530.A22. When the sample deviation rate (8.3%) exceeds the tolerable deviation rate (5%), the auditor should conclude that the control is likely not operating effectively as planned. The assessed risk of material misstatement for the affected assertion should be increased, and the auditor must expand substantive procedures accordingly. Wrong (A): A 91.7% compliance rate does not support reliance when it exceeds the tolerable threshold; the focus is on the deviation rate, not the compliance rate. Wrong (C): There is no acceptable margin above the tolerable rate; exceeding it means the sample does not support the planned reliance. Wrong (D): Control test results for one control cannot be offset against results for different controls.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q8: hard / L4 — Evaluating sequential (stop-or-go) sampling results
(1, 'Tests of Controls', $EXPL$Wallace & Partners is using sequential sampling (stop-or-go) to test an accounts receivable aging review control at Pinnacle Services. After testing the first 30 items, the auditor found zero deviations. The sampling plan indicates that with zero deviations in the initial sample, the auditor may stop testing and conclude the control is effective. However, the engagement partner has concerns about a new employee who took over the review midyear. The most appropriate action is to:$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Stop testing as the sampling plan directs, since the statistical results support effectiveness", "Continue testing additional items from the period after the new employee assumed the role to address the partner's concern about changed conditions", "Abandon the sequential approach and switch entirely to a substantive strategy", "Report the partner's concern as a scope limitation in the audit report"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): AU-C 530.A16. While the sequential sampling results technically support stopping, professional judgment requires the auditor to consider qualitative factors. A personnel change midyear introduces the risk that the control may have operated differently in the second half. Extending testing to cover the period with the new employee addresses this risk while maintaining the efficiency benefits of sequential sampling. Wrong (A): Mechanically following the sampling plan ignores the qualitative risk factor of changed personnel, which could affect control operation in the untested period. Wrong (C): Abandoning the control reliance approach entirely is premature; targeted additional testing may resolve the concern without changing the overall strategy. Wrong (D): The partner's concern is an internal audit planning matter, not a scope limitation to be reported.$EXPL$, 'hard', 4),

-- Q9: medium / L2 — Distinguishing non-statistical from statistical sampling
(1, 'Tests of Controls', $EXPL$During the audit of Wexford Corp., the senior auditor selects 25 items for a test of controls using professional judgment rather than a random number generator. The auditor evaluates the results qualitatively rather than computing a statistical confidence interval. This approach is classified as:$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Non-statistical sampling, which does not allow the auditor to project results to the population", "Statistical sampling using a simplified selection method", "Non-statistical sampling, which is acceptable provided the auditor uses professional judgment to evaluate the results", "An invalid approach because all tests of controls must use statistical sampling"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): AU-C 530.05. Non-statistical sampling uses professional judgment for both sample selection and evaluation rather than mathematical probability theory. It is an acceptable approach for tests of controls provided the auditor exercises appropriate judgment in determining sample size and evaluating results. Wrong (A): Non-statistical sampling does allow the auditor to draw conclusions about the population; the distinction is that the conclusion is based on judgment rather than mathematical precision. Wrong (B): Statistical sampling requires both a random selection method and statistical evaluation of results; judgment-based selection is non-statistical. Wrong (D): Auditing standards permit both statistical and non-statistical sampling for tests of controls; neither method is required.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q10: hard / L4 — Evaluating test data approach for IT application controls
(1, 'Tests of Controls', $EXPL$During the audit of Nexus Financial Corp., the auditor uses test data to verify that an automated edit check rejects wire transfers exceeding $1,000,000 without dual authorization. The auditor submits fictitious transactions through the production system on a weekend when no live transactions are processed. After confirming the control rejected the test transactions, the auditor must also:$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Obtain a management representation letter specifically addressing the test data results", "Verify that all test transactions were removed from the production environment to prevent contamination of live data", "Submit the same test data through a separate test environment to cross-validate the production results", "Reperform the test monthly for the remainder of the audit period to ensure ongoing effectiveness"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): AU-C 315.A120. When using test data in a production environment, the auditor must ensure that fictitious transactions are properly removed after testing to avoid contaminating production data and financial records. Failure to remove test data could introduce errors into the client's financial information. Wrong (A): While management representations may be obtained, the immediate critical step is ensuring test data removal; a representation does not address the data contamination risk. Wrong (C): Cross-validation in a test environment may be useful but is not the required follow-up step when production testing has already confirmed the control operates. Wrong (D): Monthly retesting is unnecessary for an automated control that has been confirmed to operate correctly and supported by effective ITGCs.$EXPL$, 'hard', 4),

-- Q11: easy / L1 — Definition of entity-level controls
(1, 'Tests of Controls', $EXPL$Entity-level controls are controls that operate broadly across an organization. An example of an entity-level control is:$EXPL$, $EXPL$["An automated three-way match in the accounts payable system", "A physical inventory count performed at year-end", "The board of directors' oversight of the financial reporting process", "An individual supervisor's review and approval of a specific journal entry"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): AU-C 315.A71. Entity-level controls operate at the organizational level and have a pervasive effect on the entity's system of internal control. Board oversight of financial reporting is a classic entity-level control because it affects the overall control environment rather than specific transactions. Wrong (A): An automated three-way match is a transaction-level application control within a specific process. Wrong (B): A physical inventory count is a specific transaction-level or process-level control, not an entity-level control. Wrong (D): Supervisory review of a specific journal entry is a transaction-level control focused on a particular item, not the organization as a whole.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q12: medium / L2 — Identifying block selection risk
(1, 'Tests of Controls', $EXPL$During the audit of Cascade Logistics, the auditor selects all 22 purchase orders processed during the month of March for testing. No other months are tested. This selection method is best described as block selection, and its primary limitation is that:$EXPL$, $EXPL$["It is too expensive because it requires testing every transaction in the selected period", "The auditor cannot extrapolate results to transactions processed outside the selected time period with reasonable confidence", "It always produces more deviations than random sampling methods", "It violates auditing standards because block selection is prohibited for tests of controls"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): AU-C 530.A25. Block selection (also called cluster selection) involves choosing a contiguous set of items from a single period. Its primary limitation is that the sample may not be representative of the entire audit period, making it difficult to draw conclusions about control operation in unselected months. Wrong (A): Block selection is typically less expensive than testing across all periods; cost is not its primary limitation. Wrong (C): Block selection does not inherently produce more deviations; its weakness is representativeness, not deviation frequency. Wrong (D): Block selection is not prohibited; auditing standards caution that it may not provide a sufficient basis for conclusions about the full period, but it is permitted in limited circumstances.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q13: easy / L1 — When substantive procedures alone cannot provide sufficient evidence
(1, 'Tests of Controls', $EXPL$In which situation is the auditor most likely required to test controls because substantive procedures alone are insufficient?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["When transactions are processed through a highly automated system with little manual intervention or visible audit trail", "When the entity has fewer than 50 employees", "When the entity operates in a regulated industry", "When the auditor has been engaged to perform a review rather than an audit"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): AU-C 330.08. When transactions are initiated, processed, and recorded electronically with no paper trail, it may be impractical to obtain sufficient appropriate audit evidence solely through substantive procedures. In such cases, the auditor must test the automated controls to support the integrity of the data. Wrong (B): Entity size does not determine whether substantive procedures alone are sufficient; the nature of transaction processing is the key factor. Wrong (C): Operating in a regulated industry may increase compliance risk but does not by itself make substantive procedures insufficient. Wrong (D): A review engagement does not involve testing controls or substantive procedures at the same level as an audit.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q14: easy / L1 — Corroborating inquiry with additional procedures
(1, 'Tests of Controls', $EXPL$When the auditor uses inquiry as part of testing the operating effectiveness of controls, auditing standards require that inquiry be combined with:$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Written confirmation from the entity's legal counsel", "At least one other audit procedure such as observation, inspection, or reperformance", "A statistical analysis of the control's historical deviation rate", "Approval from the audit committee before the inquiry can proceed"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): AU-C 330.A26. Inquiry alone is not sufficient to test the operating effectiveness of controls. The auditor must combine inquiry with at least one other procedure — such as observation, inspection of documents, or reperformance — to obtain sufficient appropriate evidence about how the control operated during the period. Wrong (A): Legal counsel confirmation is relevant to contingencies and litigation, not to corroborating control testing inquiries. Wrong (C): Statistical analysis of historical deviation rates may inform sample planning but is not a required corroborative procedure for inquiry. Wrong (D): There is no requirement to obtain audit committee approval before performing inquiry as a test of controls.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q15: medium / L3 — Determining testing approach for access security controls
(1, 'Tests of Controls', $EXPL$During the audit of Valence Technologies, the auditor is testing IT access security controls that restrict financial application access to authorized users. The auditor observes that the entity uses role-based access with periodic access reviews. To effectively test whether these access controls operated throughout the period, the most appropriate procedure is to:$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Inspect user access listings at year-end and compare current access rights to authorized roles for the tested applications", "Inquire of the IT director about the access control policy and accept the response as sufficient evidence", "Observe a single login attempt by an authorized user to confirm the system requires authentication", "Review the entity's written access control policy document without examining actual system configurations"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): AU-C 315.A113. Inspecting user access listings and comparing them to authorized role assignments provides evidence that access controls operated as designed. Examining access at year-end, combined with evidence of periodic reviews during the year, tests both the point-in-time state and the ongoing monitoring of access rights. Wrong (B): Inquiry alone is insufficient to test operating effectiveness; it must be combined with inspection or other corroborative procedures. Wrong (C): Observing a single login confirms the authentication mechanism exists but does not test whether access is appropriately restricted to authorized personnel. Wrong (D): Reviewing written policies tests design but not operating effectiveness; the auditor must examine actual configurations and access records.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q16: medium / L3 — Evaluating impact of a significant deficiency on the audit approach
(1, 'Tests of Controls', $EXPL$During the audit of Preston Healthcare, the auditor identifies a significant deficiency in controls over accounts payable: the three-way match is performed by the same individual who approves payments, creating a segregation-of-duties weakness. The auditor had planned a combined approach with moderate reliance on controls. The most appropriate response is to:$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Withdraw from the engagement because a significant deficiency makes the audit impossible to complete", "Increase substantive testing of accounts payable to address the higher risk resulting from the control weakness, and communicate the deficiency to those charged with governance", "Continue with the planned combined approach because a significant deficiency is less severe than a material weakness", "Rely on management's promise to correct the deficiency before year-end"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): AU-C 265.08 and AU-C 330.17. When a significant deficiency is identified, the auditor should reconsider the planned reliance on the affected controls and increase substantive procedures for the related assertions. Additionally, AU-C 265 requires written communication of significant deficiencies to those charged with governance within 60 days of the report release date. Wrong (A): A significant deficiency does not require withdrawal; the auditor modifies the approach and increases substantive testing. Wrong (C): Even though a significant deficiency is less severe than a material weakness, the auditor cannot ignore its impact on the reliability of the affected controls. Wrong (D): A promise to correct the deficiency does not address the risk during the current audit period.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q17: medium / L2 — Identifying the purpose of change management controls
(1, 'Tests of Controls', $EXPL$Dunbar & Associates, CPAs, are testing IT general controls at a client that uses a commercial ERP system. The auditor focuses on program change management controls. The primary reason the auditor tests these controls is to obtain evidence that:$EXPL$, $EXPL$["All software updates were made by the lowest-cost vendor", "Changes to programs were authorized, tested, and approved before implementation, ensuring application controls continued to function as intended", "The entity upgraded its hardware infrastructure during the year", "End users received notification of every software change made during the period"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): AU-C 315.A113. Program change management controls ensure that modifications to application software are properly authorized, tested in a separate environment, and approved before deployment. Testing these controls provides evidence that automated application controls were not improperly modified and continued to operate as designed throughout the period. Wrong (A): Cost efficiency of vendors is an operational consideration, not the purpose of change management control testing. Wrong (C): Hardware infrastructure changes relate to computer operations controls, not program change management. Wrong (D): User notification is a communication practice, not the objective of change management control testing.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q18: medium / L3 — Applying judgment to evaluate controls over estimates
(1, 'Tests of Controls', $EXPL$The auditor of Briarwood Financial is testing a control over the allowance for loan losses. The control requires the credit committee to review loan classifications and loss estimates quarterly. Testing reveals the committee met all four quarters and documented their deliberations, but the auditor notices the committee approved management's estimates without modification in every quarter. The auditor should:$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Accept the control as operating effectively because the committee met and documented all four quarterly reviews", "Consider whether the lack of any modifications raises questions about whether the committee exercised genuine independent judgment in its review", "Conclude the control failed because an effective review should always result in at least some modifications", "Report the committee's unanimous approvals as a material weakness in internal control"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): AU-C 330.A29. When testing management review controls involving significant judgment, the auditor evaluates not just whether the review occurred but whether it was performed with appropriate rigor. Unanimous approval without any modifications across four quarters may indicate a rubber-stamp process rather than genuine oversight. The auditor should investigate further before concluding on effectiveness. Wrong (A): Meeting and documenting alone do not establish that the committee performed a substantive review; form without substance is insufficient. Wrong (C): A control is not necessarily ineffective simply because no modifications were made; the estimates could have been consistently reasonable. However, the pattern warrants further investigation. Wrong (D): Lack of modifications alone does not automatically constitute a material weakness; additional evidence is needed to reach that conclusion.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q19: medium / L3 — Determining when to use parallel simulation for control testing
(1, 'Tests of Controls', $EXPL$The auditor of Quantum Processing Inc. wants to test whether an automated interest calculation control in the loan servicing system correctly computes interest on 200,000 loan accounts. The most efficient computer-assisted audit technique for testing this application control is:$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Manually recalculating interest on a sample of 25 loan accounts using a spreadsheet", "Using generalized audit software to perform parallel simulation by recomputing interest for all accounts using the contract terms and comparing results to the system output", "Observing an IT staff member process a single loan account through the system", "Sending confirmation letters to all 200,000 borrowers asking them to verify their interest charges"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): AU-C 315.A119. Parallel simulation uses generalized audit software to independently reprocess data using the same logic as the client's system. For a large-volume automated calculation, parallel simulation is the most efficient technique because it can test the entire population without relying on sampling, providing strong evidence about the application control's accuracy. Wrong (A): Manual recalculation of 25 accounts is valid but far less efficient and covers only a small fraction of the population. Wrong (C): Observing a single account provides evidence at a point in time and does not test the calculation logic across varying scenarios. Wrong (D): Confirmation letters address the external validity of balances, not the operating effectiveness of the automated calculation control.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q20: easy / L1 — Timing of written communication of control deficiencies
(1, 'Tests of Controls', $EXPL$Under AU-C 265, the auditor must communicate significant deficiencies and material weaknesses in writing to those charged with governance no later than:$EXPL$, $EXPL$["The last day of fieldwork", "30 days after the report release date", "60 days after the report release date", "The date the management representation letter is signed"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): AU-C 265.12. The auditor is required to communicate significant deficiencies and material weaknesses in writing to those charged with governance no later than 60 days following the report release date. This allows time for the communication to be prepared with appropriate care after the audit is substantially complete. Wrong (A): The last day of fieldwork is not the deadline; the standard provides additional time after the report release. Wrong (B): The deadline is 60 days, not 30 days, following the report release date. Wrong (D): The management representation letter date relates to management's assertions, not the timing of deficiency communications.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q21: medium / L3 — Applying nature-timing-extent trade-offs in control testing
(1, 'Tests of Controls', $EXPL$The auditor of Westbrook Electronics initially planned to test the purchasing authorization control by inspecting 40 purchase orders at year-end. Due to the discovery of a new fraud risk factor related to unauthorized purchases, the auditor decides to modify the planned procedures. Which modification most effectively responds to the increased risk?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Reduce the sample size to 20 items but test at both interim and year-end", "Change the nature of the test from inspection alone to a combination of inspection and reperformance, and increase the sample to 60 items tested throughout the period", "Postpone all control testing until the following year when the fraud risk factor may no longer exist", "Test the same 40 items but select only from the last month of the fiscal year"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): AU-C 330.A18. When risk increases, the auditor should respond by modifying the nature, timing, and extent of testing. Changing to more persuasive procedures (adding reperformance to inspection), increasing sample size, and testing throughout the period all enhance the quality of evidence in response to the heightened fraud risk. Wrong (A): Reducing the sample size weakens the extent of testing, which is inappropriate when risk has increased, even if testing at two points. Wrong (C): Postponing control testing does not address the current-year fraud risk and would leave the current audit without the planned evidence. Wrong (D): Concentrating selection in one month reduces the representativeness of the sample and does not respond to the increased risk.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q22: medium / L2 — Identifying continuous operation controls vs periodic controls
(1, 'Tests of Controls', $EXPL$During the audit of Clearwater Retail, the auditor distinguishes between controls that operate continuously (such as automated input validation on every sales transaction) and controls that operate periodically (such as monthly account reconciliations). This distinction primarily affects:$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Whether the auditor must issue an adverse opinion", "The number of items the auditor should test and whether ITGCs affect the testing approach", "Whether the control must be documented in the entity's policies and procedures manual", "Whether the deviation can be communicated to management"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): AU-C 330.A30 and AU-C 530.A14. The frequency and nature of a control directly affect the auditor's testing approach. Continuously operating automated controls may require fewer direct tests if supported by effective ITGCs, while periodic manual controls require testing a number of occurrences based on the total population. The distinction drives both sample size and the role of ITGC reliance. Wrong (A): The type of opinion issued depends on the pervasiveness of misstatements, not the frequency of control operation. Wrong (C): Documentation requirements for policies exist regardless of whether the control is continuous or periodic. Wrong (D): Communication requirements for deviations are the same regardless of whether the underlying control operates continuously or periodically.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q23: medium / L2 — Effect of prior-year results on current-year sample size
(1, 'Tests of Controls', $EXPL$Archer & Mills, CPAs, tested a revenue posting control in the prior year using a sample of 40 items and found zero deviations. In the current year, the auditor confirms the control has not changed and risk factors remain the same. The effect of these favorable prior-year results on the current-year sample size is that the auditor may:$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Eliminate control testing entirely because the prior year confirmed effectiveness", "Consider the favorable prior-year results as one factor supporting a smaller but still adequate current-year sample", "Double the current-year sample size to confirm the prior-year conclusion", "Rely entirely on the prior-year results and reallocate current-year testing time to other areas"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): AU-C 330.14. Favorable prior-year results, combined with no changes in the control or risk factors, may support a reduced but not eliminated current-year sample. The auditor may consider prior results as context when determining the appropriate extent of testing, but must still test some occurrences in the current period. Wrong (A): The auditor cannot eliminate testing entirely in the current year; at minimum, some controls must be tested each year. Wrong (C): Doubling the sample is inconsistent with using favorable prior results to inform the current-year approach; it would be appropriate only if risk had increased. Wrong (D): The auditor cannot rely entirely on prior-year results without any current-year testing, even when conditions are unchanged.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q24: medium / L2 — Understanding complementary user entity controls in the SOC context
(1, 'Tests of Controls', $EXPL$During the audit of Granite Construction, the auditor obtains a SOC 1 Type 2 report from the payroll service organization. The report states that the service organization's controls are effective but identifies a complementary user entity control requiring Granite to independently authorize all new employee setups before the service organization processes them. If Granite has not implemented this authorization control, the user auditor should conclude that:$EXPL$, $EXPL$["The SOC 1 report is unreliable and cannot be used for any purpose", "The service organization's controls are ineffective because Granite did not implement the CUEC", "The overall system of controls over payroll may not be effective because the assumed user entity control is not in place, increasing the risk of unauthorized employee records", "Granite is in violation of its contract with the service organization, requiring disclosure in the financial statements"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): AU-C 402.16. Complementary user entity controls are assumed to be in place by the service organization's system design. When a CUEC is not implemented, a gap exists in the overall control structure, and the user auditor should consider the risk that the uncontrolled process introduces — in this case, unauthorized employee setups could lead to payroll fraud or errors. Wrong (A): The SOC report's conclusions about the service organization's own controls remain valid; the issue is the user entity's failure to implement the assumed complementary control. Wrong (B): The service organization's controls may still be effective within their scope; the deficiency exists at the user entity level. Wrong (D): Failure to implement a CUEC is an internal control matter, not necessarily a contractual violation or financial statement disclosure issue.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q25: hard / L3 — Evaluating group auditor reliance on component auditor control testing
(1, 'Tests of Controls', $EXPL$Bradford Global Group has operations in 15 countries. The group engagement team plans to rely on controls tested by component auditors at three significant subsidiaries. Under AU-C 600, before relying on the component auditors' control testing, the group engagement team must:$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Evaluate the component auditors' competence and independence, communicate the relevant requirements, and review the component auditors' work to assess its adequacy", "Accept the component auditors' conclusions without review because they are licensed professionals in their respective jurisdictions", "Require all component auditors to use identical sampling methodologies to ensure consistency", "Perform separate, independent tests of the same controls at each subsidiary to corroborate the component auditors' findings"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): AU-C 600.19-22. The group engagement team must evaluate component auditor competence and independence, communicate the group's requirements and expectations, and review the component auditors' documentation and conclusions to determine whether their work is adequate for group audit purposes. This provides a basis for relying on their control testing. Wrong (B): The group engagement team cannot simply accept component auditor conclusions without evaluation; they retain responsibility for the group audit opinion. Wrong (C): While consistency is desirable, mandating identical sampling methodologies across all jurisdictions is not required; the focus is on adequacy of the work performed. Wrong (D): Independent retesting at every subsidiary would negate the efficiency benefit of using component auditors and is not required.$EXPL$, 'hard', 3),

-- Q26: easy / L1 — Prohibition on negative assurance about control deficiencies
(1, 'Tests of Controls', $EXPL$After completing the audit, the auditor is prohibited from issuing a written communication stating that:$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Significant deficiencies were identified during the audit", "Material weaknesses were identified during the audit", "The audit was not designed to identify all control deficiencies", "No material weaknesses were identified during the audit"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 3, $EXPL$Correct (D): AU-C 265.13. The auditor is prohibited from issuing a written communication stating that no significant deficiencies or material weaknesses were identified. Because the financial statement audit is not specifically designed to identify all deficiencies, such a statement could provide unwarranted assurance about the effectiveness of internal controls. Wrong (A): The auditor is required, not prohibited, to communicate identified significant deficiencies in writing. Wrong (B): The auditor is required, not prohibited, to communicate identified material weaknesses in writing. Wrong (C): This statement is factually accurate and may be included as context, though it is not the prohibited communication.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q27: hard / L4 — Evaluating the impact of management override on control testing strategy
(1, 'Tests of Controls', $EXPL$During the audit of Calder Industries, the auditor discovers that the CEO routinely overrides the automated purchase approval limit of $100,000 by having the system administrator increase the limit temporarily for specific transactions. The CEO then directs the administrator to reset the limit. The transactions are otherwise properly documented. In evaluating this finding, the auditor should:$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Disregard the overrides because the transactions were properly documented and the system was restored each time", "Evaluate the overrides as evidence of a control environment weakness that may affect the reliability of other controls, and consider whether the pattern indicates fraud risk factors under AU-C 240", "Report each override as an illegal act under AU-C 250 requiring immediate communication to regulators", "Accept the overrides as a normal business practice because the CEO has ultimate authority over all transactions"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): AU-C 240.31-32. Management override of controls is specifically identified as a fraud risk factor that must be addressed on every audit. Routine overrides — even when documented — indicate a weakness in the control environment because the designed control is being systematically circumvented. The auditor must evaluate whether this pattern raises concerns about management's integrity and its broader impact on other controls. Wrong (A): Proper documentation does not negate the fact that the designed control was overridden; the override itself is the concern. Wrong (C): Management override of its own controls, while a fraud risk factor, is not automatically an illegal act requiring regulatory notification. Wrong (D): While the CEO has authority, systematically circumventing designed controls undermines the internal control system and must be evaluated, not accepted.$EXPL$, 'hard', 4),

-- Q28: medium / L2 — Identifying the relationship between control risk and detection risk
(1, 'Tests of Controls', $EXPL$After completing tests of controls over sales transactions at Ridgeway Enterprises, the auditor concludes that the controls are highly effective. This conclusion directly affects the audit plan by allowing the auditor to:$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Set detection risk at a lower level and perform more extensive substantive testing", "Set detection risk at a higher level and reduce the extent of substantive testing", "Eliminate all substantive testing of sales transactions", "Increase the materiality threshold for the sales cycle only"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): AU-C 330.07. The audit risk model shows that when control risk is assessed as low (controls are effective), the auditor can accept a higher detection risk. A higher acceptable detection risk means less extensive substantive procedures are needed to achieve the desired overall level of audit risk. This is the fundamental benefit of control reliance. Wrong (A): Setting detection risk lower would increase the extent of substantive testing, which is the opposite of the appropriate response to effective controls. Wrong (C): Even when controls are highly effective, auditing standards require some substantive procedures for all material account balances and transaction classes. Wrong (D): The assessed control risk affects detection risk, not the materiality level; materiality is determined independently based on financial statement user needs.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q29: easy / L1 — Understanding the concept of control risk
(1, 'Tests of Controls', $EXPL$Control risk is the risk that a material misstatement could occur in an assertion and:$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Not be prevented or detected on a timely basis by the entity's internal controls", "Not be detected by the auditor's substantive procedures", "Exist in the financial statements due to the inherent nature of the account", "Not be identified during the engagement quality control review"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): AU-C 200.A42. Control risk is the risk that a material misstatement that could occur in an assertion will not be prevented, or detected and corrected, on a timely basis by the entity's internal control. It is a component of the risk of material misstatement and is assessed based on the auditor's evaluation of internal control effectiveness. Wrong (B): This describes detection risk, which relates to the auditor's procedures rather than the entity's controls. Wrong (C): This describes inherent risk, which exists independent of internal controls due to the nature of the account or transaction. Wrong (D): Engagement quality control review is a firm-level quality management procedure, not a component of the audit risk model.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q30: medium / L3 — Evaluating whether an embedded audit module is appropriate for continuous monitoring
(1, 'Tests of Controls', $EXPL$The auditor of Kensington Financial is considering recommending an embedded audit module in the client's loan processing system to continuously flag exceptions where loan approvals exceed authorized credit limits. Before recommending this approach, the auditor should consider that the primary advantage of an embedded audit module over periodic manual testing is:$EXPL$, $EXPL$["It eliminates the need for the auditor to perform any tests of controls", "It provides real-time exception reporting that covers all transactions processed rather than a sample, enabling continuous monitoring of the control", "It automatically corrects all exceptions without human intervention", "It reduces the cost of the audit to zero because the client's system performs the testing"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): AU-C 315.A121. Embedded audit modules are continuous monitoring routines built into the client's application that flag exceptions in real time. The primary advantage is complete population coverage with immediate detection, rather than relying on periodic sample-based testing that may miss exceptions between test dates. Wrong (A): Embedded audit modules supplement but do not replace the auditor's independent testing responsibilities; the auditor must still evaluate the module's reliability. Wrong (C): Embedded modules identify and flag exceptions for review; they do not automatically correct transactions without human intervention. Wrong (D): While embedded modules may improve efficiency, they do not eliminate audit costs; the auditor must still evaluate the module, review flagged exceptions, and perform additional procedures.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3)
;

COMMIT;
